																	<a href="/forum/t-13972958/scp-idea:cogito-ergo-sum#post-4865407">SCP Idea: Cogito, Ergo Sum</a>
									</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/blackvvater" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7113942); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7113942&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1610136208" alt="Blackvvater" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7113942)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/blackvvater" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7113942); return false;" >Blackvvater</a></span> <span class="odate time_1610135931 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 Jan 2021 19:58</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13972958/scp-idea:cogito-ergo-sum">SCP Idea: Cogito, Ergo Sum</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4865407">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre (Optional):</strong> Psychological</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout (Optional):</strong> Interview articles, &quot;Sit-in Classes&quot;</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> Students who want college credit attend a class taught by an unusual professor.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> Letters from &quot;South Euton University&quot; are sent out to high school and college students who want college credit. The class being taught is labeled &quot;Philosophy of Existence,&quot; and is taught by Professor Phillip Oliver Zofia. The classroom has an unusual setup, with strange posters and maps that have countries improperly labeled. Above the whiteboard is a large poster, upon which is written, &quot;Cogito, Ergo Sum.&quot; The professor himself is a man described as &quot;gaunt,&quot; &quot;faint,&quot; even &quot;ethereal.&quot;</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The class begins with the professor providing the students with an initial quiz. The questions are very easy (2+2=?), but the students fail when they provide standard answers. The professor then proceeds to teach the class, telling them blatantly incorrect answers, and then testing them on the incorrect knowledge. Those that refuse to answer &quot;properly&quot; are given personal instruction by Dr. Zofia. The sessions apparently take half an hour, though one student describes it feeling as though she was in there for days (vague as to whether she was or not). Invariably, the students start giving him the answers he wants, whether through exasperation or actually starting to buy into his teaching. They become increasingly suggestible as this goes on. Simultaneously, Dr. Zofia grows stronger, fuller, more substantial.</p>
<p>The final test consists of a piece of paper, asking, &quot;Cogito, ergo sum. Concur?&quot; There is also a line where the students write their names, which they invariably do. At the conclusion of the test, the students disappear, while Dr. Zofia seems to become completely rejuvenated. The SCP agent monitoring this situation (implied that Zofia is amused by their presence and is toying with them) offers them a letter and an invitation to join them next semester, and then is ushered out.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> Aside from the oddities of the classroom, the central question, &quot;I think, therefore I am,&quot; is explored. If one stops thinking, do they really exist? And if one's thoughts supercede another's, does that not make them more you, than you? This is also meant to be mild commentary on the education system caring more about correct answers than understanding material.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong></p>

