			<div class="content" id="post-content-4865363">
									

<p>The fact that the first three sentences already contains major logical and in-universe errors does not fare well to me as a reader. In fact, I have no incentive to continue reading because of these errors. Let me explain:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Reveal</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Object Class:</strong> Safe</p>
<p><strong>Special Containment Procedures:</strong> SCP 5764 is impossible to contain physically, instead, it was convinced by the foundation to stay at Site-43, in a standard cell.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why is this labeled as Safe? If this entity can pass through any walls and is &quot;impossible to contain,&quot; then it should certainly <strong>not</strong> be classified as &quot;Safe.&quot; The only explanation I can think of for this is the insinuation that &quot;Safe&quot; = &quot;Entity is physically harmless&quot; or something to that extent, which is not the Object Classes purpose. This is specified on multiple guides and even the Guide to Newbies, I believe.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>His presence is to be monitored constantly, as he roams the site's halls, passing through walls and occasionally slipping into any room imaginable, including another SCP's cell,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What does &quot;any room imaginable&quot; even quantify towards? Why <em>imaginable</em>? This isn't appropriate clinical tone whatsoever. This could've been easily rectified by: &quot;SCP-5764's presence is be continually monitored as it is capable of freely transversing Site-XX with no visible restictions.&quot; (&lt;&#8212; Although even something like this provides a multitude of logical and in-universe errors. Why is this being allowed? Why hasn't the Foundation moved or convinced the anomaly to be moved towards another secure area? The list goes on)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[&#8230;] these accidents only take mere moments to solve however, and no lasting negative effects on the staff or the scps has been detected.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence is complex, choppy, and severely grammatically flawed. This could have been rectified by a simple grammar checker or a reviewer. In fact, most of these errors could have probably been fixed if you had someone else look at it before you posted it.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
The fact that this doesn't even bother being logically or grammatically accurate doesn't make me want to read it whatsoever. The anomaly itself is also conceptually weak, constituting towards a pretty lackluster OP entity that I have no interest in due to the aforementioned problems listed above. Next time, I highly suggest you pay attention the the Guides and Essays as well as using the Forums (i.e. the Idea Critique Forum or the Draft Critique Forum) before you publish future articles. I promise, they're there for a reason.
							</div>

								
					
